 "title": "A CNN trained on Fashion-MNIST in 43s of CPU",
 "prescribes": "Write the loop explicitly: model.train() / model.eval() around each phase, torch.no_grad() for evaluation, gradient clipping, a scheduler stepped once per epoch, AMP only on CUDA, and checkpoints saved on validation improvement.",
 "applied": "Trained a 0.43M-parameter CNN for 6 epochs on a 12,000-image Fashion-MNIST subsample, evaluating on 3,000 held-out images each epoch.",
 "narrative": [
  "Best validation accuracy is 89.400% at epoch 6, reached in 43 seconds on CPU (torch 2.5.1+cpu -- this is the CPU build, so AMP is disabled and the autocast/GradScaler path is present but inert; the same loop runs unchanged on CUDA).",
  "The mode discipline is not decoration. Running the identical evaluation with the model left in `train()` -- dropout active, batchnorm updating on test batches -- gives 87.433% instead of 89.400%. That is a +1.967% swing from one forgotten line, and nothing crashes to tell you.",
  "Per-class accuracy exposes what the aggregate hides: Shirt is the weakest class at 70.7%, and its errors go mostly to T-shirt/top. Shirt/pullover/coat confusion is the known hard case in this dataset, not a bug in the loop.",
  "Checkpointing writes only when validation improves and stores the optimiser state and the seed alongside the weights, so training is resumable rather than merely restartable."
 ],
